  "strategy": "We aim to find a sequence of length 1800 with minimal autocorrelation power.\n Our approach combines genetic algorithms for initial structure discovery, smooth interpolation for sequence length adjustment, and \n optimization via projected subgradient and Frank-Wolf methods with adaptive scheduling.\n Steps:\n 1. Start with uniform population of 100 sequences of length 1000, project to simplex space.\n 2. Perform multi-generational evolution with fitness measured by the given evaluation function.\n 3. Use interpolation to map the best sequence to length 1800.\n 4. Apply projected subgradient optimization and coordinate descent for minimal improvement.\n 5. Monitor constraints and objective values to ensure progress.\n\n The algorithm:\n - Initialize population as 100 disturbed uniform sequences of length 1000.\n - For each generation, evaluate all sequences, choose parents via tournament selection, \n   perform crossover and mutation, then project to simplex space.\n - Use interpolation to map the best sequence of length 1000 to length 1800.\n - Apply refinement: project subgradient optimization with line-search to minimize the convolution peak.\n\n Detailed steps:\n 1. Initialization:\n    Start with parent sequence (if provided) normalized to length 1000.\n\n 2.Genetic Algorithm:\n    For generation in range(generations):\n        - Evaluate fitness via the given function.\n        - Select parent pairs via tournament selection.\n        - Perform crossover (exchange coefficients) with probability p_crossover.\n        - Apply mutation (random walk) with probability p_mutation.\n        - Project generated sequence to simplex space.\n        - Keep the best individual.\n\n 3.Interpolation:\n    If time allows, interpolate between the best GA individual and uniform to get length 1800.\n\n 4.Refinement:\n    Use projected subgradient method (with Nesterov acceleration) to find the minimal improvement for sequences of increasing length from 1000 to 1800.\n\n 5.Divergence Check:\n    If the GA finds a sequence that destabilizes the objective (ratio > 1.6), fall back to the interpolation candidate.\n\n 6.Time Management:\n    Run for up to 3600 seconds (6 minutes), but we are given a budget of 1800 seconds.\n\n The implementation uses:\n   - np.convolve for autocorrelation.\n   - np.argsort, np.random for crossover/mutation.\n   - Projected gradient for line-search.\n\n Since 1800 seconds is not extremely long, we use:\n\n   gens = 100\n   pop_size = 100\n\n   n0 = 1000 (target length for GA)\n\n   Steps: \n      Create population: uniform distribution (1/n0 for each coefficient)\n      Jitter the population: random perturbations for diversity.\n\n   Then:\n      For each candidate: clip values and normalize.\n      Evaluate with given evaluate_sequence().\n\n   We use tournament selection (size=5) and uniform crossover.\n   Mutation: Gaussian jitter clipping to 0 and 1000.\n\n Mathematical details:\n   The objective is 2*n*max(conv)/sum(a)^2.\n   We minimize this, with sum(a) \u2265 0.01.\n\n Complete sequence:\n\n   We'll return the sequence from refinement if it improves, else the best GA candidate.\n\n Let's code accordingly.",
